  "texts": [
    {
      "title": "Source",
      "pos": [
        -120,
        120
      ],
      "text": "Drawn from General Guitar Gadgets' \"Modernized Marshall Vintage BluesBreaker Replica\"\nschematic (2018-08-29), refs and values as there; its true bypass and LED left out.\nhttps://generalguitargadgets.com/pdf/ggg_mbb_sc.pdf"
    }
  ],
  "probes": {
    "in": "in",
    "out": "out"
  },
  "notes": {
    "part:R1": "Input pull-down.",
    "part:IC1A": "First stage: a non-inverting gain stage. Its feedback is Gain's top half (wiper to pin 1); its legs to ground go through C3, and C2 with R3, so the gain falls away in the bass.",
    "part:VR1": "Gain: turning it up moves resistance from the second stage's input (C6, R8 side) into the first stage's feedback, so both stages gain at once.",
    "part:R2": "R2 and R3 (with C2) are the first stage's legs to ground: GGG notes some factory units had 27k and 33k here (less gain, more bass), as Aion's V1 mode.",
    "part:C4": "Takes the first stage's gain down at the top of the band; GGG notes some factory units had 10p.",
    "part:C6": "With R8, GGG's single coupling cap and resistor where the original has two of each with the footswitch's mute between them (see the acceptance notes).",
    "part:IC1B": "Second stage: an inverting stage, gain R10 / (R8 + Gain's bottom half), with the soft clipping in its feedback.",
    "part:D2": "Soft clipping: two diodes each way in series with R11, across R10, so the stage's gain falls as they conduct.",
    "part:VR2": "Tone: a treble cut, C8 to VB through the pot, then R12 and C9.",
    "part:VR3": "Volume, between the tone network and VB; C10 blocks VB's DC from the output and R13 holds the output at ground.",
    "part:D1": "Reverse-polarity protection, in series with the supply."
  },
  "enclosure": {
    "size": "125B"
  }
}
